      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **compound**, meaning it is made by chemically bonding two different elements together.\n*   **Oxygen** is highly electronegative, meaning it pulls electrons toward itself.\n*   This creates a \"bent\" molecular shape where the oxygen end has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge.\n*   This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter at standard temperatures and pressures on Earth:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking, bathing, and swimming.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible steam).\n\n### 3. Key Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients and waste in living organisms.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, which allows small insects to walk on water.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth (keeping the planet from getting too hot or too cold) and stabilizes the internal temperature of living things.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. It is the primary component of all known forms of life and is essential for:\n*   Chemical reactions in cells.\n*   Lubricating joints and organs.\n*   Transporting nutrients and oxygen through the bloodstream.\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting."
